.Dd $Mdocdate$
.Dt NFDUMP.CONF 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nfdump.conf
.Nd configuration file for the nfdump flow-tools suite
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Pa %prefix/etc/nfdump.conf
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is the shared configuration file for all programs in the nfdump suite:
.Xr nfdump 1 ,
.Xr nfcapd 1 ,
.Xr sfcapd 1 ,
.Xr nfpcapd 1 ,
and
.Xr nfanon 1 .
It is written in
.Sy TOML
(Tom's Obvious Minimal Language) format.
.Pp
The file is optional.
If it is absent no error is reported; each program falls back to its
compiled-in defaults.
.Sh OPTION PROCESSING ORDER
Each configurable parameter is resolved through the following priority
chain, from lowest to highest:
.Bl -enum
.It
.Sy Program defaults
\(em values compiled into the program binary.
These apply when nothing else overrides them.
.It
.Sy Config file
\(em the file opened by
.Fl C
or the default path, parsed at start-up.
A value here overrides the compiled-in default.
.It
.Sy CLI override
\(em a
.Fl x Ar key Ns = Ns Ar value
argument on the command line.
This takes precedence over both the config file and the program default
and is effective only for the duration of the process.
.El
.Sh FILE FORMAT
.Nm
uses
.Sy TOML 1.0
syntax.
Lines beginning with
.Sq #
are comments.
Keys and values are separated by
.Sq = .
String values must be enclosed in double quotes.
Integer and boolean values are unquoted.
.Pp
The file is divided into named
.Em sections
enclosed in square brackets.
Keys defined inside a section apply only to the program that reads that
section.
Keys defined in the special
.Sx [common]
section apply to all programs and serve as a shared fallback.
.Sh SECTIONS
.Ss [common]
Settings in
.Sy [common]
are read by every program in the suite.
A program-specific section takes precedence over
.Sy [common]
when the same key appears in both.
.Ss [nfdump]
Settings for
.Xr nfdump 1 .
.Ss [nfcapd]
Settings for
.Xr nfcapd 1 .
.Ss [sfcapd]
Settings for
.Xr sfcapd 1 .
.Ss [nfpcapd]
Settings for
.Xr nfpcapd 1 .
.Ss [nfanon]
Settings for
.Xr nfanon 1 .
.Ss [nfmeta]
Settings for
.Xr nfmeta 1 .
.Sh KNOWN KEYS
.Ss Common keys (valid in [common] or any program section)
.Bl -tag -width "crypt.rekeyIntervalSecs"
.It Sy xxhash
Boolean integer (0 or 1).
When set to 1, an xxHash checksum is appended to each written data block
and verified on every read.
Detecting silent data corruption comes at a small CPU cost.
Default: 0.
.It Sy limitCores
Integer.
Sets the core limit when no
.Fl W
flag is given on the command line.
The value 0 (or the absent key) means
.Dq use all online CPU cores .
.Sy GetThreadConfig()
then splits the core limit across readers, writers, and filter/worker threads
according to the program role and the active compression codec.
A non-zero
.Sy limitCores
caps the budget without overriding individual role counts.
Prefer the
.Sx [threads]
sub-table keys for fine-grained per-role control.
.Pp
The legacy key name
.Sy maxworkers
is still accepted as a fallback: if
.Sy limitCores
is absent or 0, the code falls back to
.Sy maxworkers .
New configurations should use
.Sy limitCores .
.Pp
Both supersede the previous behaviour where the default was
.Li cores/2
clamped to [2,\~8].
.It Sy crypt.salt
16-byte string.
Shared salt used for encrypted UDP data transfer between
.Xr nfpcapd 1
or
.Xr nfcapd 1
and a receiving collector.
Both sender and receiver must use the same value.
.It Sy crypt.antiReplayWindowBits
Integer.
Size of the anti-replay window for encrypted UDP transport, as a power of 2
in the range [64,\~1024].
Default: 256.
.It Sy crypt.rekeyIntervalSecs
Integer.
Interval in seconds at which the session key is rotated.
Set to 0 to disable epoch rekeying.
Default: 0.
.El
.Ss [threads] sub-table keys (valid in [common.threads] or [program.threads])
The
.Sy threads
sub-table refines how
.Sy GetThreadConfig()
allocates threads within the budget.
All values default to 0, meaning the role formula is applied.
A non-zero value is a hard override that bypasses the formula entirely.
These keys are accepted inside any section's
.Sy threads
sub-table, for example
.Li [common.threads] ,
.Li [nfdump.threads] ,
or
.Li [nfanon.threads] .
They can also be set per-run via the command line:
.Fl x Ar threads.writers=6 .
.Bl -tag -width "threads.writers"
.It Sy threads.readers
Integer.
Number of nfreader decompression threads launched per input file.
When 0, the count is derived at file-open time as
.Li round(ref\~/\~C) ,
where
.Em ref
is
.Sy writers
for TC_ROLE_TRANSFORM programs (nfanon, nfmeta) or
.Sy filters
for TC_ROLE_ANALYZE programs (nfdump, nfprofile, nftrack), and
.Em C
is the codec's compress/decompress time ratio
(LZ4\~\(ap\~2, ZSTD\~\(ap\~5, BZ2\~\(ap\~3, LZO\~\(ap\~2.5).
Default: 0 (auto).
.It Sy threads.writers
Integer.
Number of nfwriter compression threads per output file.
For TC_ROLE_WRITE_ONLY programs (nfcapd, sfcapd, nfpcapd) the budget is
used in full, capped at a codec-specific ceiling:
LZ4\~\(->8, ZSTD\~\(->12, uncompressed\~\(->2.
The cap leaves one core for the collector's hot-path thread and avoids
L3 cache saturation under LZ4.
For TC_ROLE_TRANSFORM and TC_ROLE_ANALYZE the budget is shared among roles,
so the effective writer count is proportionally lower.
Default: 0 (auto).
.It Sy threads.workers
Integer.
Number of application worker threads.
The meaning depends on the program role:
.Bl -tag -width "TC_ROLE_ANALYZE" -compact
.It Sy TC_ROLE_ANALYZE
Filter and aggregation workers in
.Xr nfdump 1 ,
.Xr nfprofile 1 ,
.Xr nftrack 1 .
These are the primary CPU bottleneck.
.It Sy TC_ROLE_TRANSFORM
Anonymization workers in
.Xr nfanon 1
or bloom-indexing workers in
.Xr nfmeta 1 .
.It Sy TC_ROLE_WRITE_ONLY
Unused (always 0 regardless of this setting).
.El
Default: 0 (auto).
.El
.Ss [nfdump] keys
.Bl -tag -width "geodb.flatpath"
.It Sy geodb.path
String.
Path to the compiled nfdump MaxMind GeoIP2 database file (see
.Xr geolookup 1 ) .
.It Sy geodb.flatpath
String.
Path to the flat-file cache directory for the geoDB, used to speed up
repeated lookups.
.It Sy tordb.path
String.
Path to the compiled nfdump Tor exit-node database (see
.Xr torlookup 1 ) .
.It Sy tordb.flatpath
String.
Path to the flat-file cache directory for the Tor database.
.It Sy fmt. Ns Ar name
String.
Define or redefine a named output format.
.Ar name
is the format identifier used with
.Fl o Ar fmt: Ns Ar name .
The value is a format string as described in
.Xr nfdump 1 .
.It Sy csv. Ns Ar name
String.
Define or redefine a named CSV output format, used with
.Fl o Ar csv: Ns Ar name .
.El
.Ss [nfcapd] keys
.Bl -tag -width "exporter.ident"
.It Sy exporter. Ns Ar ident
Array of two strings: the exporter IP address and the flow directory.
Multiple exporters may be defined by using different
.Ar ident
values.
.El
.Ss [sfcapd] keys
.Bl -tag -width "opt.tun"
.It Sy opt.tun
Boolean integer (0 or 1).
Enable decoding of tunnelling protocols (GRE and 6-in-4).
Default: 0.
.El
.Ss [nfpcapd] keys
.Bl -tag -width "opt.payload"
.It Sy opt.fat
Boolean integer (0 or 1).
Capture extended flow information (FAT flows).
Default: 0.
.It Sy opt.payload
Boolean integer (0 or 1).
Capture packet payload data.
Default: 0.
.It Sy buffSize
Integer.
BPF capture buffer size in bytes on BSD systems.
Must be at least 32768 and should be a multiple of the system page size
(4096).
Default: 1048576 (1\~MB).
.El
.Ss [nfanon] keys
TC_ROLE_TRANSFORM: 20% of the budget is reserved for anonymization workers;
the remainder is split between nffile readers and writers by codec ratio.
Use
.Sy threads.workers
to override the worker count directly.
.Ss [nfmeta] keys
TC_ROLE_TRANSFORM: same 20% split as nfanon.
Bloom-filter updates are fast relative to LZ4 compression, so the default
is almost always sufficient.
Use
.Sy threads.workers
to override the worker count directly.
.Sh PROGRAM DEFAULTS
Each program has a set of compiled-in default values for its own keys.
These defaults are active even when no configuration file is present.
They represent the lowest priority in the option resolution chain:
a value found in the configuration file or supplied via
.Fl x
always takes precedence.
.Pp
The defaults for the boolean program options are:
.Bl -tag -width "opt.payload (nfpcapd)"
.It Sy opt.tun Pq sfcapd
0 (disabled)
.It Sy opt.fat Pq nfpcapd
0 (disabled)
.It Sy opt.payload Pq nfpcapd
0 (disabled)
.El
.Sh CONFIGURATION FILE SELECTION
Programs search for the configuration file in the following order:
.Bl -enum
.It
The file given with
.Fl C Ar file .
If the special value
.Sq none
is passed to
.Fl C ,
configuration-file processing is skipped entirely.
.It
The path in the environment variable
.Ev NFCONF .
.It
The compiled-in default path
.Pa %prefix/etc/nfdump.conf .
If that file does not exist, no error is reported and program defaults
are used.
.El
.Sh CLI OVERRIDES
Any configuration key can be overridden at run time without modifying the
configuration file.
Use the
.Fl x Ar key Ns = Ns Ar value
option, which is accepted by all programs in the suite.
The option may be repeated to override multiple keys:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nfdump -x limitCores=4 -x xxhash=1 -r data.nf
.Ed
.Pp
.Ar key
must be the exact dot-separated key path as it would appear in the
configuration file.
.Ar value
is always interpreted as a string and converted to the appropriate type
when the key is read (integer via
.Xr strtoll 3 ,
boolean as non-zero integer).
.Pp
If
.Ar key
is not present in the configuration file and has no compiled-in default,
a warning is logged but the override is accepted and remains effective
for the duration of the process.
.Pp
Overrides are processed after the configuration file is opened, so they
take effect for every key lookup that follows.
They do not modify the configuration file on disk.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Ss Minimal common settings
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[common]
xxhash = 1
limitCores = 4
.Ed
.Ss nfdump with custom formats and GeoIP
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[nfdump]
geodb.path = "/var/db/mmdb.nf"
tordb.path = "/var/db/tordb.nf"

# redefine the built-in 'line' format
fmt.line = "%ts %td %pr %sap -> %dap %pkt %byt %fl"

# add a custom format
fmt.compact = "%ts %pr %sap -> %dap %byt"
.Ed
.Ss nfcapd with named exporters
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[nfcapd]
exporter.upstream = ["192.168.1.1",  "/var/nflow/upstream"]
exporter.peer     = ["192.168.1.10", "/var/nflow/peer"]

[nfcapd.threads]
writers = 6
.Ed
.Ss nfpcapd with payload capture enabled
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[nfpcapd]
opt.fat     = 1
opt.payload = 1
buffSize    = 2097152
.Ed
.Ss Encrypted UDP transfer
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[common]
crypt.salt                = "my-secret-salt-16"
crypt.antiReplayWindowBits = 512
crypt.rekeyIntervalSecs   = 300
.Ed
.Ss Thread tuning
Cap nfdump filter workers at 8 and fix writer count to 4 regardless of cores:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[nfdump.threads]
filters = 8
writers = 4
.Ed
.Pp
On a 64-core server running nfcapd with LZ4, the automatic cap is 8 writers.
Override to allow more writers (with matching SSD throughput):
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[nfcapd.threads]
writers = 12
.Ed
.Pp
Fix the anonymization worker count when CryptoPAN runs without AES-NI
(on a 16-core host, 4 workers instead of the default 3):
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[nfanon.threads]
filters = 4
.Ed
.Pp
Per-run override without modifying the file \(em fix 6 writers for one nfdump run:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nfdump -x threads.writers=6 -r data.nf -w out.nf
.Ed
.Ss Overriding a single key from the command line
Enable block checksums for one run without touching the config file:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nfdump -x xxhash=1 -r data.nf -w output.nf
.Ed
.Pp
Override limitCores and an unknown key (the latter produces a warning):
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nfcapd -x limitCores=4 -x debug.verbose=1 -w /var/nflow
.Ed
.Ss Disable config file entirely
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nfdump -C none -r data.nf
.Ed
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width "NFCONF"
.It Ev NFCONF
If set, its value is used as the path to the configuration file when no
.Fl C
option is given.
Takes precedence over the compiled-in default path.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "%prefix/etc/nfdump.conf.dist"
.It Pa %prefix/etc/nfdump.conf
Default configuration file location.
.It Pa %prefix/etc/nfdump.conf.dist
Annotated example configuration distributed with the package.
Copy this file to
.Pa nfdump.conf
and uncomment the desired settings as a starting point.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr nfdump 1 ,
.Xr nfcapd 1 ,
.Xr sfcapd 1 ,
.Xr nfpcapd 1 ,
.Xr nfanon 1 ,
.Xr nfmeta 1 ,
.Xr geolookup 1 ,
.Xr torlookup 1
